      "affectedModernTexts": [
        {
          "index": 2,
          "text": "Here r is the distance from the center of the sphere to the observation point. Outside the sphere, the field-line pattern is the same as that of a point charge at the center with the same total charge, as shown in Figure 126. A matching line pattern alone does not determine field magnitude: the same total charge and spherical symmetry are also needed. In a homogeneous surrounding medium, the external field is therefore given by the point-charge formula (8-13). The source writes $r\\geq R_0$; at the surface, the following expression means the limiting field approached from outside. In SI units,"
        },
        {
          "index": 8,
          "text": "At electrostatic equilibrium, the field inside the metal sphere, where $r<R_0$, is zero. The permittivity factors in the preceding formula are the vacuum permittivity and the relative permittivity of the surrounding medium, respectively."
        },
        {
          "index": 11,
          "text": "Consider an ideal infinite, uniformly charged thin plane with the same homogeneous medium on both sides. Its field lines are parallel and perpendicular to the plane, as shown in Figure 127. The field is uniform within each half-space, and its magnitude is independent of distance from the plane. The magnitudes on the two sides are equal, but the directions are opposite. Lines point away from positive charge and toward negative charge. The magnitude is determined by the surface charge density $\\sigma$."
        },
        {
          "index": 15,
          "text": "In SI units, with surface charge density measured in coulombs per square meter,"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
